## measured
#   hold                13:40:40Z  expires 14:40:40Z
#   task deleted        13:40:52.003   (absent from the 13:41:01.073 sample on)
#   task absent for     13:40:52 -> 13:45:32 = 4 m 40 s, 28 consecutive empty samples
#   RECREATED           started_at 13:45:32Z / expires 14:45:32Z, first seen 13:45:41.557
#   recreation stamp    exactly 13:45:32Z = Hub B start 13:35:32 + 2 x 300 s
#   operation="sprites.hold-refresh" 0 -- the PUT answered success, no error was reported
#
# This also pins the tick period independently: refreshes landed at 13:35:32 (recovery),
# 13:40:32 (no-op, the run was fired 8 s later) and 13:45:32, i.e. exactly 300 s apart.
#
# NOTE the sprite was unheld for those 4 m 40 s. It did not pause only because task-poll.sh execs
# every 10 s, and an exec wakes a sprite. That is a property of the measurement, not of Hub: with
# no poller the sprite would have paused mid-turn, which is exactly the ceiling docs/sprite-targets.md
# section 7 states ("a Hub outage longer than the remaining expiry lets the sprite pause mid-turn;
# the turn resumes on the next hold"). What is proved here is the recreation, not the no-pause.
